WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:02.440
- Thanks to Trade Coffee for
keeping Legal Eagle in the air,

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:03.700
'cause you would not want to see me

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:06.170
before my first cup of Joe. Am I right?

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:09.680
Courage Gallegos asks,
are you a Sith Lord?

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:11.445
Wouldn't you like to know?

00:00:11.445 --> 00:00:14.960
(playful music)

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:15.793
Hey, Legal Eagles.

00:00:15.793 --> 00:00:18.270
It's time to celebrate actually,

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:20.460
because just a little while ago,

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:23.220
this channel crossed a million subscribers

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:24.710
and roughly at the same time,

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:27.290
it crossed a hundred
years million video views.

00:00:27.290 --> 00:00:28.870
I'm absolutely floored.

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:31.450
I can't believe that this
channel that was started

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:34.250
as a hobby, just because
I got mad at the TV

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:38.250
when people were getting the
law wrong on TV and movies

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.890
has expanded into a channel where

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:43.130
a million of you have signed up

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:47.810
to hear more about the law
and to learn more about

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:50.250
how to think like a lawyer
and to learn more about

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:52.590
the laws that surround us every day.

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:55.900
So from the bottom of my
heart, thank you so much.

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:59.470
It's been an honor and a
pleasure to create this channel,

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:01.620
and I'm so honored that so
many of you have decided

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:02.900
to come along for the ride.

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:06.260
And since I can't throw a big
party where I can invite all

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:10.230
of you for various incredibly
obvious reasons right now,

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:12.220
I thought the best way
that we could celebrate

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:14.040
is to celebrate all of you guys,

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.200
because I couldn't have
done this without you.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.070
So I asked for the questions
that you might have

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:21.960
about Legal Eagle the
channel, about me personally,

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.150
or about life in general.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.190
So let's answer some of those questions

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:29.660
and talk about just what it
is you guys want to know.

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:31.840
The first question is from Nate Myers,

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:34.630
who asks "in what ways
has your YouTube channel

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:37.190
affected your job, your life in general?"

00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:40.200
As if there's any distinction
between my life and my job,

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:41.800
those are definitely one in the same.

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:44.748
So in terms of how the YouTube
channel has affected the job,

00:01:44.748 --> 00:01:48.913
not all that much, actually,
day to day practicing law.

00:01:49.841 --> 00:01:51.790
The fact that I have a YouTube channel

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:53.440
doesn't really change very much.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:56.480
Sometimes I get recognized,
but usually not at all.

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:59.270
And frankly, the main way
that the YouTube channel

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:02.490
has affected my professional life is that

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.460
it just takes a huge amount of time

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:06.160
to be able to do these videos.

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:07.840
I think that every video,

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:10.830
if we're talking about a
substantive legal issue

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:15.530
takes roughly 10 to 20 hours
to research and outline,

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:19.880
another one to four hours to shoot,

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:23.550
and then another 10 to 20 hours to edit

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:26.650
and review and edit again
and go back and forth.

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:29.460
Now I'm not doing the
video editing anymore,

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:31.510
but still that's a huge amount
of time to sort of go back

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:33.040
and supervise and, and that sort of stuff.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:34.350
So for better or worse,

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:37.100
the YouTube channel is
starting to crowd out

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:39.850
my professional, legal and business life.

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:41.720
So that's definitely
something I have to take into

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:45.310
consideration, but it's
something that I absolutely love.

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:48.880
I have the best job in the
world to have this channel

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:52.920
and to be able to make creative videos.

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:55.390
I mean, semi creative, they're
about the law after all,

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:58.090
but to be able to make these videos

00:02:58.090 --> 00:02:59.960
as part of my professional life

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:02.943
is just the best thing in the world.

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:05.700
And it's a lot of hard work,

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:08.810
but it's also incredibly
fulfilling and satisfying as well.

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:09.867
Kieran O'Sullivan asks

00:03:09.867 --> 00:03:11.930
"Does the fact that most
criminal cases are settled

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:13.110
with plea deals bother you,

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:16.000
given the power imbalance
between the defendant and state?"

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.610
I'd say yes and no.

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:20.230
There's always going to be
a power imbalance between

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:23.060
the government, the state, and the people,

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:26.240
especially individuals
and especially people who

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:30.593
tend to be in lower
socioeconomic conditions.

00:03:30.593 --> 00:03:35.258
I think that we can and often
have done things that help

00:03:35.258 --> 00:03:37.530
make it a little bit more fair.

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:40.940
Under the Gideon case,
all criminal defendants

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:43.070
are entitled to representation.

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:44.640
That's what public
defenders are there for.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:47.280
I think that public
defenders do a great service

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.030
to individuals and to the country

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:51.170
and they should receive more funding.

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:55.100
And the fact that things are often settled

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:58.020
through plea deals is not
necessarily the worst thing.

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:01.370
We don't want to have to spend
huge amounts of resources

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:04.610
on criminal trials every
time there is an accusation

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:06.910
of criminal conduct, obviously
there are going to be times

00:04:06.910 --> 00:04:09.970
when people take a plea deal because

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:11.250
it's the expedient thing to do

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:14.550
or because they're not willing
to fight it out in court,

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:16.540
but often that's just
a more efficient way.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:19.360
People are going to get less
of a criminal sentence through

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:21.510
a plea deal than they would
if they took things to court.

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:23.570
So no system is perfect.

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:26.170
Certainly the American
criminal justice system

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:27.846
is far from perfect,

00:04:27.846 --> 00:04:31.100
but to the extent that there is injustice,

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:33.210
it's up to all of us to make it better.

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:34.043
And we certainly can.

00:04:34.043 --> 00:04:35.490
There's a lot of things that we can do,

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:37.310
and we've talked about
that on this channel.

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:38.660
There's a lot that we can and I think will

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:39.493
be done in the future.

00:04:39.493 --> 00:04:42.180
The MovieSquad asks, "have
you ever received criticism

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:44.660
from other lawyers for
hosting a YouTube channel?"

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:47.220
Well, first of all, I have
so many lawyer friends

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:49.650
and they're all very
supportive of the channel,

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:53.410
and a few people have expressed
to me that Legal Eagle

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:55.480
is the channel that they
wish they had started.

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:57.020
So ha, beat you to it.

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:58.770
That being said in real life,

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:02.870
the support is pretty unanimous,
and for the most part,

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:07.130
I think people recognize
that I am trying to explain

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:11.008
fairly uncontroversial
legal issues in the news

00:05:11.008 --> 00:05:14.397
and that legal literacy is something that,

00:05:14.397 --> 00:05:16.890
for better or worse, a lot of people need.

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:20.420
That being said, online, I
have received lots of criticism

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:25.220
from political pundits,
from other attorneys

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:27.750
who may or may not have
done their research,

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:32.090
and from a lot of people who
claim to be attorneys online.

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:34.960
I am a little suspicious
of a lot of those comments

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:37.030
that claim to have done the research

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:40.233
or have studied this
topic for dozens of years.

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:44.474
But yeah, for the most
part, I think the criticism

00:05:44.474 --> 00:05:49.474
is generally well-meaning or
just incredibly misinformed.

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:53.740
Michaela P asks, "who is
the we at Legal Eagle?

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:55.840
How big is the team in the background?"

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:59.820
So I am so, so lucky to
have a few individuals

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:03.410
who help me out every day and frankly,

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:06.280
where this channel is
now would not be possible

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:08.070
without the people that I work with.

00:06:08.070 --> 00:06:11.060
I have two main video
editors, Amy and Chris,

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:15.710
who do a fantastic job, and
often on insane deadlines.

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:19.330
I am constantly amazed at
how quickly Chris and Amy

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:22.430
can turn out some of
these videos and frankly,

00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:23.650
how polished everything is.

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:26.410
If you see the 2D graphics
that are on the screen

00:06:26.410 --> 00:06:29.660
and they'll fly in and fly out,
or the full screen graphics,

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:31.510
I have no idea how to do that.

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:32.650
That's all motion graphics

00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:34.370
that's done in Adobe After Effects.

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:36.570
I can edit together a video if I need to,

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:40.570
but I am totally incapable of
doing what Amy and Chris do.

00:06:40.570 --> 00:06:41.760
I just simply give them comments

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:43.834
and tell them that I want more of this

00:06:43.834 --> 00:06:46.350
(angelic music)

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:47.397
or less of that,

00:06:47.397 --> 00:06:50.160
(mumbling incoherently)

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:51.160
and they make it happen.

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:54.350
In addition to that, I work
with two amazing attorneys,

00:06:54.350 --> 00:06:58.160
Andrea and Rob, who often do a
huge amount of legal research

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:02.500
when there is a topic where I'm
not a subject matter expert.

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:05.200
For example, the video I
did on Posse Comitatus,

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:09.290
that is not a law that I deal
with on an everyday basis.

00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:12.670
And there's like one guy who
has ever done any research

00:07:12.670 --> 00:07:14.290
on that particular topic.

00:07:14.290 --> 00:07:16.560
Often I need help with
some of the research,

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:18.900
and Andrea and Rob are incredibly amazing

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:22.600
when it comes to helping out
with actually researching

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:26.000
these sometimes very arcane
and archaic laws that,

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:28.280
because of the times that we live in,

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:31.375
somehow rear their ugly head
and we have to figure out

00:07:31.375 --> 00:07:35.430
what's going to happen and
what the legal confines are

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:36.980
of the various situations
that we deal with.

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:39.230
I also work with some amazing animators,

00:07:39.230 --> 00:07:42.290
Alex and Nick, who are
responsible largely for

00:07:42.290 --> 00:07:44.490
the animated series "The Case Of"

00:07:44.490 --> 00:07:46.530
that deals with various
true crime related things.

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:47.930
So that's the lion's share of the team

00:07:47.930 --> 00:07:50.890
over here at Legal Eagle.
I'm hoping to expand.

00:07:50.890 --> 00:07:52.900
I'm hoping to be able to do more videos,

00:07:52.900 --> 00:07:55.500
which may require that
the team get even larger.

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:58.320
Definitely would not
be possible by myself,

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:01.260
and I'm so indebted to having
such an incredible team,

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:03.340
and I'm looking forward
to working with them

00:08:03.340 --> 00:08:04.490
in the future on this.

00:08:04.490 --> 00:08:07.550
Asha'man Rich asks
"everyone out here asking

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:10.010
hard hitting questions, and
I'm interested in one thing,

00:08:10.010 --> 00:08:13.180
pineapple on pizza, yay or nay, and why?"

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:15.880
Wow, man. I mean, that is a
hard hitting question too.

00:08:15.880 --> 00:08:19.850
I do enjoy a pizza with
pineapple on it on occasion.

00:08:19.850 --> 00:08:22.540
I would say I much prefer
generally speaking,

00:08:22.540 --> 00:08:26.600
a Neapolitan pizza with added onions.

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:28.930
I'm a onion on pizza guy,

00:08:28.930 --> 00:08:31.920
but there's definitely
a time and a place for

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:34.440
a pizza with a little
sweetness added to it.

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:35.380
Got no problem with that.

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:37.327
All right, Nik Hunder asks

00:08:37.327 --> 00:08:40.110
"sometimes you have to
represent the bad guy in a case.

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:42.050
In those situations,
how do you stay neutral

00:08:42.050 --> 00:08:44.170
in order to best represent your client?"

00:08:44.170 --> 00:08:47.440
So I actually sort of
disagree with the premise

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:49.980
of this particular question.

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:52.740
A lot of my career has been spent in

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:56.140
the civil side of things, the
vast majority of it, in fact,

00:08:56.140 --> 00:09:00.260
and the majority of that
is spent on civil defense,

00:09:00.260 --> 00:09:01.980
although I have spent
a fair amount of time

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:04.060
doing civil plaintiff work as well.

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:07.029
I was definitely prepared
coming out of law school

00:09:07.029 --> 00:09:10.930
to represent someone who was a bad guy,

00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:14.520
or more likely someone who was wrong.

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:17.670
What I found actually is
that in every single case

00:09:17.670 --> 00:09:22.390
I ever had, all of my clients,
and really if I'm fair,

00:09:22.390 --> 00:09:25.370
the other people that we were up against,

00:09:25.370 --> 00:09:27.800
had colorable legal arguments.

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:30.193
If you file a lawsuit or
where you have to deal with

00:09:30.193 --> 00:09:33.286
a lawsuit or you're
getting into litigation,

00:09:33.286 --> 00:09:36.620
odds are the case is
somewhere on the margins,

00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:40.350
such that each side is going
to have reasonable arguments.

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:43.080
And when you have reasonable arguments,

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:47.750
it's easy to see why your
client should win the case.

00:09:47.750 --> 00:09:49.360
Now, sometimes those legal arguments

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:51.120
are more of a technicality.

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:55.040
Frankly, if a client was
clearly in the wrong,

00:09:55.040 --> 00:09:58.597
then often it's the role
of the lawyer to tell them

00:09:58.597 --> 00:10:01.670
that they should not fight
this particular case.

00:10:01.670 --> 00:10:05.567
Often that's why the
vast majority of cases

00:10:05.567 --> 00:10:07.760
actually settle out of court.

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:11.900
So if there was a rare instance
where I didn't feel like

00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:15.750
the client had a leg to stand on,

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:19.646
then almost certainly that
client would have settled

00:10:19.646 --> 00:10:22.360
and gotten out of the lawsuit that way.

00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:25.440
If you get into multimillion
dollar litigation

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:28.240
and you keep going forward
and you get to trial,

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:29.780
the reason that you're in trial is

00:10:29.780 --> 00:10:31.950
because both sides think they can win.

00:10:31.950 --> 00:10:33.610
So that's actually the rarity,

00:10:33.610 --> 00:10:36.640
to actually get that far into a case.

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:40.550
For the most part, if
people did something wrong,

00:10:40.550 --> 00:10:41.970
they're going to settle
and try and get out

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:43.280
as quickly as possible.

00:10:43.280 --> 00:10:45.870
Sergei Niskanen says, "how
can one become a judge?

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:47.560
Are there some special
law schools for that?

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:49.490
Or is it more a question
of long-term legal practice

00:10:49.490 --> 00:10:51.240
and general experience, or maybe there are

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:52.450
some very specific requirements.

00:10:52.450 --> 00:10:55.340
Have you ever considered a
judicial career for yourself?"

00:10:55.340 --> 00:10:56.670
So how you become a judge?

00:10:56.670 --> 00:10:58.610
Well, that depends on the jurisdiction.

00:10:58.610 --> 00:11:00.650
Some are appointed by the
governor or the president,

00:11:00.650 --> 00:11:03.567
and some are elected,
depends on where you live

00:11:03.567 --> 00:11:05.160
and the particular court
you're dealing with.

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:08.260
No special law schools,
any law school will do,

00:11:08.260 --> 00:11:10.660
though going to a higher ranked law school

00:11:10.660 --> 00:11:12.950
makes you more likely to become a judge,

00:11:12.950 --> 00:11:14.010
I would probably say.

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:16.140
Though, when you are
appointed to be a judge,

00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:18.810
often certain states
and the federal system

00:11:18.810 --> 00:11:20.960
will send you to judge
school because often

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:22.030
people don't know what they're doing

00:11:22.030 --> 00:11:24.700
and it's sort of a crash course
and actually being a judge.

00:11:24.700 --> 00:11:27.600
So technically there is sort
of a special judge school,

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:29.940
and I have absolutely no aspirations

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:31.350
of becoming a judge myself.

00:11:31.350 --> 00:11:34.160
I am very, very thankful that
we have a lot of good judges

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:36.380
out there, but that is
definitely not something

00:11:36.380 --> 00:11:37.670
I would want to do myself.

00:11:37.670 --> 00:11:40.120
All right, let's do some
rapid fire questions.

00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:42.000
I think I've been taking
this way too seriously,

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:43.880
and I want to get through a
bunch more of these questions.

00:11:43.880 --> 00:11:45.947
So let's try and do it.
Christopher Miller asks,

00:11:45.947 --> 00:11:47.530
"when did it become comfortable

00:11:47.530 --> 00:11:49.060
speaking in front of a judge?"

00:11:49.060 --> 00:11:51.900
I have never had a problem
speaking in front of a judge.

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:56.590
I think that dates back to
doing speech and debate in high

00:11:56.590 --> 00:11:58.930
school, and actually
frankly in middle school

00:11:58.930 --> 00:11:59.763
and maybe elementary school,

00:11:59.763 --> 00:12:01.050
I did a bunch of speech competition.

00:12:01.050 --> 00:12:03.010
So I have always been very,

00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:04.710
very comfortable speaking
in front of a crowd.

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:06.470
And you know, a judge
is just like a crowd,

00:12:06.470 --> 00:12:08.870
but it's one person and
they're very, very powerful.

00:12:08.870 --> 00:12:11.070
Okay. Zane Woollett asks
"when can I get a t-shirt

00:12:11.070 --> 00:12:12.470
that says the bailiff will tackle you?"

00:12:12.470 --> 00:12:15.500
I don't know, I'm not
totally sold on merch yet.

00:12:15.500 --> 00:12:17.730
Maybe some day, let me
know if you guys want that,

00:12:17.730 --> 00:12:21.910
or like tie clips or,
I'm very particular about

00:12:21.910 --> 00:12:24.530
a very special kind of legal pad,

00:12:24.530 --> 00:12:28.057
so if you guys would want
Legal Eagle legal pads,

00:12:28.057 --> 00:12:30.310
let me know, maybe merch is in the future,

00:12:30.310 --> 00:12:31.390
I could definitely make that happen.

00:12:31.390 --> 00:12:33.700
Kalp Shah asks, "why
did you become a lawyer

00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:35.290
and then stop to do YouTube?

00:12:35.290 --> 00:12:37.650
Seems to me a lawyer
would be an easier job."

00:12:37.650 --> 00:12:39.300
Well, I haven't stopped being a lawyer.

00:12:39.300 --> 00:12:41.270
I still practice all the time,

00:12:41.270 --> 00:12:43.758
though YouTube is definitely
taking up a larger and larger

00:12:43.758 --> 00:12:45.740
portion of my day to day life,

00:12:45.740 --> 00:12:49.470
which is awesome because YouTube
is really, really creative.

00:12:49.470 --> 00:12:53.630
Who gets to make creative
video essays all day long?

00:12:53.630 --> 00:12:56.620
You know, being a lawyer
is great, I love it.

00:12:56.620 --> 00:12:58.780
I always wanted to be a lawyer, but also,

00:12:58.780 --> 00:13:00.890
the deadlines are
terrible in the sense that

00:13:00.890 --> 00:13:02.260
sometimes if you you miss a deadline,

00:13:02.260 --> 00:13:05.587
your client is screwed, and
to live under the Damocles of

00:13:05.587 --> 00:13:09.120
these looming deadlines,
man, that is no fun.

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:12.001
At least in YouTube, if
you miss a video by a day

00:13:12.001 --> 00:13:14.760
is not jurisdictional and you
get kicked off of YouTube.

00:13:14.760 --> 00:13:16.820
That can happen in real life as a lawyer.

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:20.177
So, you know, YouTube is
fun. Tri Monster asks,

00:13:20.177 --> 00:13:22.350
"What is a part of your
job that people typically

00:13:22.350 --> 00:13:24.390
don't realize lawyers do?"

00:13:24.390 --> 00:13:27.840
I would say probably
telling them not to sue,

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:31.340
or that a lawsuit is not
in their best interest,

00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:34.810
or telling them that they don't want me to

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:37.850
actually go forward and be
as aggressive as possible.

00:13:37.850 --> 00:13:41.230
A lot of people see lawyers
on TV and they assume that

00:13:41.230 --> 00:13:43.890
the best thing to do is always sue

00:13:43.890 --> 00:13:45.650
and to always be incredibly aggressive.

00:13:45.650 --> 00:13:47.750
That's actually rarely the case,

00:13:47.750 --> 00:13:50.320
and sometimes something
is not worth suing over.

00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:51.430
You might have been wronged,

00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:53.980
but it's not worth it to
spend tens of thousands,

00:13:53.980 --> 00:13:56.120
if not hundreds of thousands
of dollars on attorneys.

00:13:56.120 --> 00:13:59.020
Sometimes there are amicable
ways of settling things

00:13:59.020 --> 00:14:02.480
that are actually net
positives for both sides.

00:14:02.480 --> 00:14:07.020
And so a lot of them lawyer's
job is not just as a zealous

00:14:07.020 --> 00:14:12.020
advocate, but also as a business
person to help two sides

00:14:12.350 --> 00:14:16.820
come together to find a
mutually amicable solution.

00:14:16.820 --> 00:14:18.517
Let's see, Jonathan Chang asks,

00:14:18.517 --> 00:14:21.260
"when you doing the collab with Dr. Mike?"

00:14:21.260 --> 00:14:23.090
When Dr. Mike responds.

00:14:23.090 --> 00:14:25.870
If I need to stand
outside of his apartment

00:14:25.870 --> 00:14:27.990
with a boombox over my
head like John Cusack

00:14:27.990 --> 00:14:30.410
to make that happen, I
will totally do that.

00:14:30.410 --> 00:14:31.500
Dr. Mike is awesome.

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:32.640
You should definitely watch his channel,

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:35.050
and I hope someday we get to collaborate.

00:14:35.050 --> 00:14:38.520
Courage Gallegos asks,
"are you a Sith Lord?"

00:14:38.520 --> 00:14:40.120
Wouldn't you like to know?

00:14:40.120 --> 00:14:41.047
All right, Susanna asks,

00:14:41.047 --> 00:14:43.190
"I am a high school
student and have considered

00:14:43.190 --> 00:14:44.950
becoming a lawyer, but I'm unsure.

00:14:44.950 --> 00:14:46.860
What are some ways I can
know if being a lawyer

00:14:46.860 --> 00:14:48.530
is the right career I should go into?"

00:14:48.530 --> 00:14:49.480
Well, a couple of things.

00:14:49.480 --> 00:14:51.150
Number one, you can do mock trial in high.

00:14:51.150 --> 00:14:53.780
I did a mock trial all
throughout high school,

00:14:53.780 --> 00:14:55.400
college and law school.

00:14:55.400 --> 00:14:57.390
While it's not the greatest representation

00:14:57.390 --> 00:14:59.090
of the day to day life of a lawyer,

00:14:59.090 --> 00:15:01.625
it will give you a taste for litigation

00:15:01.625 --> 00:15:03.930
and it will make you a
better public speaker,

00:15:03.930 --> 00:15:05.800
and it will help you think on your feet.

00:15:05.800 --> 00:15:08.760
In addition to that, pay
attention in English class.

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:12.230
What lawyers really do every day is write.

00:15:12.230 --> 00:15:13.600
They write and write and write.

00:15:13.600 --> 00:15:16.030
They'll do some research and
then they'll write some more.

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:19.540
And so being a lawyer is more
about being a great writer

00:15:19.540 --> 00:15:22.660
than it is being a great public
speaker, generally speaking.

00:15:22.660 --> 00:15:24.970
If that appeals to you, then law school

00:15:24.970 --> 00:15:26.670
might be the way to go.

00:15:26.670 --> 00:15:30.600
Pedro4816 asks, "Are you going
to read all of the comments,

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:32.820
including this one?" Yes.

00:15:32.820 --> 00:15:36.890
Idoit asks "since you've looked
at a lot of legal movies,

00:15:36.890 --> 00:15:38.940
what do you think makes
a legal movie enjoyable

00:15:38.940 --> 00:15:40.710
while still being accurate?"

00:15:40.710 --> 00:15:43.330
While I am happy to criticize legal movies

00:15:43.330 --> 00:15:46.055
for their accuracy, legal accuracy is not

00:15:46.055 --> 00:15:48.560
the most important thing for me

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:50.210
when I'm watching a legal movie,

00:15:50.210 --> 00:15:53.330
I think the dialogue needs to
be good, and most importantly,

00:15:53.330 --> 00:15:55.260
the characters need to grow.

00:15:55.260 --> 00:15:56.840
Basically everything
that happens in the plot

00:15:56.840 --> 00:16:00.700
is really just in service
of helping the protagonist

00:16:00.700 --> 00:16:03.130
or the antagonist grow as a person,

00:16:03.130 --> 00:16:05.320
and that I think is the
most important thing.

00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:07.580
Sometimes legal accuracy
totally gets in the way of that,

00:16:07.580 --> 00:16:10.320
but sometimes you'll get a
show like Better Call Saul

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:12.580
that is able to tell amazing stories

00:16:12.580 --> 00:16:14.020
while still being legally accurate,

00:16:14.020 --> 00:16:15.460
and that's super cool too.

00:16:15.460 --> 00:16:17.670
CoolGuyNotFoolGuy asked "stupid question,

00:16:17.670 --> 00:16:19.860
but why did you choose
the name Legal Eagle?"

00:16:19.860 --> 00:16:22.950
Because Eagles are cool and it rhymes.

00:16:22.950 --> 00:16:24.680
And as one of the comments pointed out,

00:16:24.680 --> 00:16:28.390
unlawful falafel doesn't
have the same ring to it.

00:16:28.390 --> 00:16:30.560
Okay, Edward Zhang asks
"to add to the question

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:32.240
of your specialty in
law, what made you decide

00:16:32.240 --> 00:16:33.500
on this particular specialty?

00:16:33.500 --> 00:16:35.570
I'm a prospective law student
and having a hard time

00:16:35.570 --> 00:16:37.330
figuring out what I
want to specialize in."

00:16:37.330 --> 00:16:39.917
Well, in a way, I haven't
really specialized.

00:16:39.917 --> 00:16:42.290
I am a litigator and trial lawyer,

00:16:42.290 --> 00:16:44.640
so I guess my specialty is the process,

00:16:44.640 --> 00:16:48.550
the process of the lawsuit,
the process of going to trial,

00:16:48.550 --> 00:16:51.030
but in terms of the substantive
law that I deal with,

00:16:51.030 --> 00:16:54.510
it has varied widely.
It includes copywriting,

00:16:54.510 --> 00:16:56.100
includes class action defense,

00:16:56.100 --> 00:16:59.930
it includes contracts,
business, IP, banking,

00:16:59.930 --> 00:17:03.250
and it's great that every
day I get to research

00:17:03.250 --> 00:17:04.940
something new, it keeps things fresh.

00:17:04.940 --> 00:17:06.940
And that's one of the things
I love about this particular

00:17:06.940 --> 00:17:10.220
channel is that I get to do research into

00:17:10.220 --> 00:17:13.860
new parts of the law to
become a momentary expert

00:17:13.860 --> 00:17:16.480
about these particular
things so that I can explain

00:17:16.480 --> 00:17:17.590
the law to other people.

00:17:17.590 --> 00:17:20.670
So in some ways, I
haven't had to specialize,

00:17:20.670 --> 00:17:22.760
and that's one of the
things that I think is great

00:17:22.760 --> 00:17:25.210
about being a litigator
and/or a trial lawyer,

00:17:25.210 --> 00:17:28.920
Bren S asks, "What was your
proudest moment as a lawyer?"

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:31.810
There was one case
where I was dealing with

00:17:31.810 --> 00:17:33.640
the worst opposing counsel

00:17:33.640 --> 00:17:35.720
I have ever dealt with in my entire life.

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:39.660
Just the biggest jerk I've
ever had the misfortune

00:17:39.660 --> 00:17:42.680
to go up against, and when I
beat the pants off of that guy,

00:17:42.680 --> 00:17:44.350
I was really, really happy about that

00:17:44.350 --> 00:17:45.550
because he deserved to lose

00:17:45.550 --> 00:17:48.050
and he deserved to get
smacked down by the court.

00:17:48.050 --> 00:17:50.920
If I remember correctly, he
was sanctioned by the court,

00:17:50.920 --> 00:17:52.410
which doesn't happen all that often.

00:17:52.410 --> 00:17:55.680
It's a little petty, but
man, not going to deny,

00:17:55.680 --> 00:17:56.800
it felt really, really good.

00:17:56.800 --> 00:18:00.760
Olivia KOREVAAR asks, "what is
an important thing about law

00:18:00.760 --> 00:18:02.660
you wish everyone understood?"

00:18:02.660 --> 00:18:05.200
This is going to sound
strange coming from a lawyer,

00:18:05.200 --> 00:18:08.800
but just because something
is legal or illegal,

00:18:08.800 --> 00:18:12.150
doesn't mean it's morally
right or morally wrong,

00:18:12.150 --> 00:18:13.820
which is one of the reasons why law

00:18:13.820 --> 00:18:16.140
is so bound up in politics.

00:18:16.140 --> 00:18:18.400
The end result of politics is law.

00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:21.190
We always have to stay
vigilant to make sure that

00:18:21.190 --> 00:18:26.070
our laws reflect our society and justice.

00:18:26.070 --> 00:18:28.140
Okay. Jonathan Chang asks,
"How old are you, man?

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:28.973
I've always wondered that."

00:18:28.973 --> 00:18:30.850
I am 36. Thank you for asking.

00:18:30.850 --> 00:18:32.650
Valerio asks "if you
hadn't become a lawyer,

00:18:32.650 --> 00:18:34.630
what other profession/job
would you have had

00:18:34.630 --> 00:18:35.650
or like to do?"

00:18:35.650 --> 00:18:36.740
Before I wanted to be a lawyer,

00:18:36.740 --> 00:18:38.630
there was a very brief amount of time

00:18:38.630 --> 00:18:40.530
where I wanted to be an architect.

00:18:40.530 --> 00:18:44.180
In my spare time, I often do
a bunch of home renovations.

00:18:44.180 --> 00:18:47.350
I really like the idea of
playing with space and, you know,

00:18:47.350 --> 00:18:50.320
making your home nicer. So I
think if I wasn't a lawyer,

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:53.599
I'd probably be architect or a contractor

00:18:53.599 --> 00:18:57.950
doing renovation stuff, which
is kind of a hobby of mine.

00:18:57.950 --> 00:19:01.350
And finally I_pancake asks "what
led you to doing YouTube in

00:19:01.350 --> 00:19:03.650
addition to being a lawyer?" Funny story.

00:19:03.650 --> 00:19:05.260
So when I was in high school,

00:19:05.260 --> 00:19:09.670
my senior English teacher
gave us the option to either

00:19:09.670 --> 00:19:14.670
write an essay or to do a final
project as a video project.

00:19:15.229 --> 00:19:18.250
Well, hello there, I'm Devin Stone.

00:19:18.250 --> 00:19:21.830
me and my three best friends
thought writing an essay

00:19:21.830 --> 00:19:22.840
was sounded terrible.

00:19:22.840 --> 00:19:25.770
So we decided to do this
video project that turned into

00:19:25.770 --> 00:19:28.120
this huge thing that became an hour long,

00:19:28.120 --> 00:19:30.420
where we satirized every
piece of English literature

00:19:30.420 --> 00:19:32.270
that we read that year in class.

00:19:32.270 --> 00:19:33.830
Sorry, sweetheart.

00:19:33.830 --> 00:19:35.742
I have another mistress.

00:19:35.742 --> 00:19:37.560
(scoffs)

00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:40.300
Her name is justice.

00:19:40.300 --> 00:19:42.000
I'm actually still really proud of this.

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:44.850
We did Macbeth as celebrity Jeopardy.

00:19:44.850 --> 00:19:47.620
Once again, his is Alex
Trebek for Celebrity Jeopardy

00:19:47.620 --> 00:19:50.083
saying good night, good night.

00:19:50.083 --> 00:19:52.916
(dramatic music)

00:19:54.170 --> 00:19:57.790
we Did Jane Austen's Pride
and Prejudice as a soap opera

00:19:57.790 --> 00:19:59.460
called The Proud and The Prejudiced.

00:19:59.460 --> 00:20:00.970
I think it's still really good.

00:20:00.970 --> 00:20:04.250
So that really got me
interested in video editing,

00:20:04.250 --> 00:20:07.230
and I didn't really pick it
up again until much later.

00:20:07.230 --> 00:20:10.100
I wanted to start a channel
where I helped law students

00:20:10.100 --> 00:20:11.160
get through law school,

00:20:11.160 --> 00:20:12.970
because I had a really
good time in law school,

00:20:12.970 --> 00:20:15.390
I thought it was really fun,
and it had a lot of tips

00:20:15.390 --> 00:20:17.910
and tricks for people to
do well in law school.

00:20:17.910 --> 00:20:20.280
So I started Legal Eagle as a way to help

00:20:20.280 --> 00:20:21.930
current and future law students,

00:20:21.930 --> 00:20:25.360
and that sort of snowballed
into what you see today,

00:20:25.360 --> 00:20:28.080
where I expanded it not just
to tips about law school,

00:20:28.080 --> 00:20:30.090
but just explaining the law in general.

00:20:30.090 --> 00:20:33.120
So that's how I got started with YouTube.

00:20:33.120 --> 00:20:36.620
It's been a crazy ride and I
guess I might as well tell you

00:20:36.620 --> 00:20:38.330
what's next for the channel.

00:20:38.330 --> 00:20:40.980
For the near future, we're
going to be doubling down.

00:20:40.980 --> 00:20:44.770
There are so many legal issues
that we have to discuss,

00:20:44.770 --> 00:20:46.490
so many things that happen in the news,

00:20:46.490 --> 00:20:50.230
and I would really like to
be able to do more videos.

00:20:50.230 --> 00:20:53.880
Over the last few months,
given that everyone has been

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:55.360
sort of confined to their house,

00:20:55.360 --> 00:20:58.330
I've been able to find some
time and get more videos out,

00:20:58.330 --> 00:20:59.940
which has helped the channel grow,

00:20:59.940 --> 00:21:02.420
and I would like to be able to
do that on a permanent basis.

00:21:02.420 --> 00:21:04.100
If not two videos a week,

00:21:04.100 --> 00:21:06.980
then perhaps three videos every two weeks,

00:21:06.980 --> 00:21:09.680
I'd like to expand out
different formats of videos.

00:21:09.680 --> 00:21:12.280
I have a bunch of ideas
of things that are related

00:21:12.280 --> 00:21:15.090
to the channel, but I haven't
been able to touch on yet,

00:21:15.090 --> 00:21:17.030
maybe some more humorous content.

00:21:17.030 --> 00:21:19.340
And if you have ideas
about how you'd like to see

00:21:19.340 --> 00:21:22.170
that expansion going, I'm all ears.

00:21:22.170 --> 00:21:23.830
And like I said, it's so much fun

00:21:23.830 --> 00:21:26.560
to be able to do these
videos, and it's so rewarding

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:28.488
to know that that some people

00:21:28.488 --> 00:21:30.900
find this information valuable.

00:21:30.900 --> 00:21:33.780
Of course, people ask
me how I have the time

